--TEST--
A class's own `parse` in a `try`, whose `catch` sets the variable to `null`, gives the value or `null` where the class has no `tryParse` — `rule:expressions/a-cheaper-form-with-the-same-result-is-compiled-or-named`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `Port::parse` returns a port number from 1 to 65535, and throws an error for anything else.
// `Port` has no `tryParse` method.
final class Port {
    public int $number;

    public function constructor(int $number) {
        $this->number = $number;
    }

    public static function parse(string $s): Port {
        int $n = $s as int;
        if ($n < 1 || $n > 65535) {
            throw new RuntimeError("not a port: " . $s);
        }
        return new Port($n);
    }
}

final class Ports {
    public static function read(string $s): string {
        ?Port $v = null;
        try {
            $v = Port::parse($s);
        } catch (RuntimeError) {
            $v = null;
        }
        return $v == null ? "null" : $v->number as string;
    }
}

// `"8080"` and `"1"` are ports. `"0"` and `"70000"` are out of range, and `"web"` is not a number.
array<string> $inputs = ["8080", "1", "0", "70000", "web"];
foreach ($inputs as string $s) {
    echo Ports::read($s), "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
8080
1
null
null
null
